# examples/apache/planetgen.conf.example
#
# Example Apache2 virtual host for the planetGen database browser
# (docs/html-interface.md documents the application itself) and the
# read-only JSON API (docs/api.md) that now lives alongside it under the
# same DocumentRoot. Copy this file to
# /etc/apache2/sites-available/planetgen.conf, edit ServerName/logging as
# needed, then enable it:
#
#   sudo a2enmod cgid wsgi headers
#   sudo a2ensite planetgen
#   sudo systemctl reload apache2
#
# `cgid` (the threaded MPM-safe CGI module) is preferred over the older
# `cgi` module; either works, but `cgid` is required if apache2 is running
# mpm_event or mpm_worker (the Ubuntu default), since plain `mod_cgi` only
# supports mpm_prefork. `wsgi` (`libapache2-mod-wsgi-py3` on Debian/
# Ubuntu) serves the API (`html/wsgi.py`) -- skip that module and the
# WSGIScriptAlias block below if you only want the CGI browser. `headers`
# is needed for the `Header always set ...` lines below regardless of
# which of those two you use.
#
# Expected layout on the server (see the repo's set-permissions.sh, which
# assumes this same layout):
#
#   /var/lib/planetGen/
#     src/html/  -- this vhost's DocumentRoot (the repo's src/html/
#                   directory), api/ included (moved there from src/api/
#                   so the API deploys from the same tree instead of a
#                   second location)
#     src/, ...  -- the rest of the repo checkout (src/stellarObjects/ in
#                   particular), so src/html/'s scripts can import the
#                   planetGen package without a separate `pip install`
#
# The database itself is a MySQL server, not a local file under this
# layout (TODO.md Phase 5) -- point the CGI scripts and the API at it with
# the PLANETGEN_MYSQL_* SetEnv directives below.
#
# /api/ (mod_wsgi): every page under html/ now fetches its data from the
# planetGen API (docs/api.md) instead of querying MySQL directly (see
# docs/html-interface.md) -- PLANETGEN_API_BASE_URL (default
# "http://127.0.0.1/api", i.e. this same vhost) is where each CGI script
# looks for it; no override needed for the common case of API and browser
# sharing one vhost the way this example does. WSGIScriptAlias below
# mounts html/wsgi.py's `application` at / -- the API under /api/ and the
# HTML pages that have moved off CGI everywhere else -- in its own daemon
# process (WSGIDaemonProcess) rather than Apache's embedded/shared-process
# mode. Needs `a2enmod wsgi alias` (alias is on by default).

<VirtualHost *:80>
    ServerName planetgen.example.com
    DocumentRoot /var/lib/planetGen/src/html

    # Defense-in-depth response headers applied to every response from
    # this vhost -- CGI pages, the API, and static/ alike. html/api/app.py
    # and html/lib/page.py already set the same ones (plus a stricter,
    # surface-specific Content-Security-Policy of their own) on their own
    # responses; this is a floor underneath both, not the only place
    # they're decided, so static/'s plain CSS/JS gets them too.
    Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
    Header always set X-Frame-Options "DENY"
    Header always set Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"

    # Tell the CGI scripts which MySQL server/account to use. Only needed
    # if any differs from stellarObjects._db.MySQLConfig's own defaults
    # (127.0.0.1:3306, user/database "planetgen"). Use a SELECT-only
    # database account here -- neither the web interface nor the API's
    # read endpoints ever write (see docs/api.md's "Write endpoints" --
    # those are stubs today regardless of account).
    #
    # This only configures the CGI browser above, NOT the mod_wsgi API
    # below: mod_cgi(d) copies SetEnv values into each CGI script's real
    # process environment, but mod_wsgi only exposes them through the
    # per-request WSGI `environ` dict, never `os.environ` -- and
    # html/api/config.py reads PLANETGEN_MYSQL_* from `os.environ` once,
    # at process startup, before any request (and its `environ` dict) has
    # ever existed. To point the API at a different database, export the
    # same variables in the Apache service's own process environment
    # instead (e.g. `/etc/apache2/envvars`, or an `Environment=` line on
    # the apache2 systemd unit) -- every mod_wsgi interpreter inherits
    # that, unlike SetEnv.
    # SetEnv PLANETGEN_MYSQL_HOST db.example.com
    # SetEnv PLANETGEN_MYSQL_PORT 3306
    # SetEnv PLANETGEN_MYSQL_USER planetgen_readonly
    # SetEnv PLANETGEN_MYSQL_PASSWORD changeme
    # SetEnv PLANETGEN_MYSQL_DATABASE_PREFIX planetgen
    #
    # The 3D Galaxy Map's tile cache (html/lib/tilecache.py). Defaults to
    # /var/cache/planetgen/tiles, which install.sh/update.sh create for
    # Apache's user. If you move it here rather than in config.json's
    # tile_cache.dir, create it with:
    #   sudo examples/apache/create-cache-dir.sh /path/to/tiles
    # SetEnv PLANETGEN_TILE_CACHE_DIR /var/cache/planetgen/tiles
    # SetEnv PLANETGEN_TILE_CACHE_MAX_MB 200
    #
    # NOTE: none of the SetEnv lines on this page reach the mod_wsgi API
    # below (see the WSGIScriptAlias block's own comment) -- set every
    # PLANETGEN_* variable (including the two below) in Apache's own
    # process environment instead (e.g. /etc/apache2/envvars) for the API
    # to see it at all.
    #
    # No separate write-capable account -- the API's write endpoints
    # (sector/system create/update/delete, and everything under
    # /api/auth/) reuse PLANETGEN_MYSQL_* above, so it needs INSERT/
    # UPDATE/DELETE grants too if those endpoints are reachable, not just
    # SELECT. See docs/apache-deployment.md#mysql-accounts.
    #
    # The control schema (admin logins/sessions/API keys/audit log) --
    # defaults to "planetgen_control" if unset.
    # SetEnv PLANETGEN_CONTROL_DATABASE planetgen_control

    # URL routing (the pages are moving from CGI to the Flask app, one
    # at a time -- docs/html-interface.md, "Flask pages"):
    #
    #   /static/...   Apache serves the files directly (Alias below).
    #   /<name>.py    a page still on CGI (ScriptAliasMatch below).
    #   everything else -- /, /sectors, /systems, /api/..., and each page
    #                 as it moves -- goes to the Flask app (html/wsgi.py,
    #                 WSGIScriptAlias / further down).
    #
    # mod_wsgi runs its WSGIScriptAlias after mod_alias's Alias/
    # ScriptAliasMatch, so these two win for the paths they match even
    # though WSGIScriptAlias / matches every URL. The regex only matches a
    # top-level `name.py` of lowercase letters and underscores (the CGI
    # pages), never wsgi.py and never anything under lib/ or api/, which
    # therefore fall through to Flask and get its 404 page.
    Alias /static/ /var/lib/planetGen/src/html/static/
    ScriptAliasMatch "^/((?!wsgi\.py$)[a-z_]+\.py)$" "/var/lib/planetGen/src/html/$1"

    <Directory /var/lib/planetGen/src/html>
        Options +ExecCGI -Indexes
        AddHandler cgi-script .py
        Require all granted
    </Directory>

    # html/wsgi.py isn't a CGI script -- it's imported by mod_wsgi, never
    # executed as one -- but <Directory html> above's `AddHandler
    # cgi-script .py` still applies to it too, since it's a .py file
    # directly inside that same directory. In production this handler
    # conflict won, not WSGIScriptAlias below: every /api/* request (and
    # a direct /wsgi.py request) got routed through mod_cgid instead of
    # mod_wsgi, which tried to exec() wsgi.py as a script and failed with
    # "AH01215: (8)Exec format error: exec of '.../wsgi.py' failed" (it
    # has no #! shebang line, since it's not meant to run standalone under
    # CGI). SetHandler forces the handler unconditionally, unlike
    # AddHandler, which only sets it when one hasn't already been chosen
    # -- overriding the CGI handler here regardless of processing order.
    #
    # A previous version of this block instead did `Require all denied`
    # here, meant to block a direct (non-aliased) request for /wsgi.py
    # from falling through to the CGI handler -- but <Files> matches the
    # final resolved filename regardless of whether the URL got there via
    # WSGIScriptAlias or a plain path, so that denied every /api/*
    # request too, not just direct ones.
    <Files "wsgi.py">
        SetHandler wsgi-script
    </Files>

    # html/lib/ holds shared Python modules imported by the CGI scripts,
    # not pages of their own -- deny it outright rather than relying on
    # the .py handler alone (belt-and-suspenders: a misconfigured handler
    # would otherwise let it execute, or a future non-.py file in there
    # could be served as plain text).
    <Directory /var/lib/planetGen/src/html/lib>
        Require all denied
    </Directory>

    # static/ is plain CSS/JS -- served directly, never as CGI.
    <Directory /var/lib/planetGen/src/html/static>
        Options -ExecCGI -Indexes
        Require all granted
    </Directory>

    # Mounts the Flask API (docs/api.md) at /api, handled by mod_wsgi via
    # html/wsgi.py rather than the CGI handler above -- relies on the
    # `<Files "wsgi.py"> SetHandler wsgi-script` override above to win out
    # over <Directory html>'s CGI handler (see that block's own comment).
    # Runs in its own daemon process (WSGIDaemonProcess), not
    # Apache's embedded/shared-process mode, for real isolation from the
    # CGI browser's own requests. html/api/ itself holds only importable
    # Python source (never requested directly), so it gets the same
    # deny-all belt-and-suspenders treatment as html/lib/ above.
    # WSGIDaemonProcess must come before WSGIScriptAlias: mod_wsgi
    # validates that a WSGIScriptAlias's process-group=... already names a
    # declared daemon process at the point it parses that line, not by the
    # end of the file -- declaring the group after (or via a directive
    # later in the file) fails apache2ctl configtest with "WSGI process
    # group not yet configured", even though the block reads fine top to
    # bottom otherwise.
    #
    # Keep `processes=1` unless PLANETGEN_RATELIMIT_STORAGE_URI (see
    # html/api/config.py) is also set to a shared backend (e.g.
    # redis://...): Flask-Limiter's default in-memory storage only counts
    # requests correctly within a single process, and `create_app` logs a
    # warning at startup if that assumption stops holding. `threads=5`
    # alone shares this one process's memory, so it stays accurate.
    #
    # request-timeout=60 restarts the daemon if requests run far longer
    # than any page waits for them (the CGI pages give up after 30 s), so
    # a runaway request can't hold threads and memory until the kernel's
    # OOM killer takes Apache down with it -- what happened on 2026-09-24
    # with the Galaxy Map's old unbounded view query.
    WSGIDaemonProcess planetgen-api processes=1 threads=5 request-timeout=60
    #
    # Mounted at / (not /api): the same app serves the JSON API under /api
    # and the moved HTML pages everywhere else. Replace an older
    # `WSGIScriptAlias /api ...` line with this one; don't keep both.
    WSGIScriptAlias / /var/lib/planetGen/src/html/wsgi.py process-group=planetgen-api application-group=%{GLOBAL}
    <Directory /var/lib/planetGen/src/html/api>
        Require all denied
    </Directory>

    ErrorLog ${APACHE_LOG_DIR}/planetgen_error.log
    CustomLog ${APACHE_LOG_DIR}/planetgen_access.log combined
</VirtualHost>

# --- HTTPS ---
# The `:80` VirtualHost above serves plain HTTP; a production deployment
# should terminate TLS in front of it rather than serve real traffic this
# way. Simplest path: `certbot --apache`, which rewrites the `:80` block
# into a redirect and generates the `:443` block below for you -- but
# read the warning below regardless of whether you do this by hand or let
# certbot do it, since certbot's own rewrite has the exact same trap.
#
# *** Whatever redirects :80 to :443 MUST exclude /api. ***
#
# `html/lib/apiclient.py` (every CGI page's own HTTP client for the API)
# defaults to PLANETGEN_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1/api -- a plain-HTTP
# loopback call made on EVERY page load (every page calls it at least
# twice: its own data, plus /api/auth/me for the nav bar's login state).
# A blanket "redirect everything on :80 to https" rule (including
# certbot's own default rewrite) catches that loopback call too: Python's
# urllib follows the redirect, turning what should be an instant local
# call into a real round trip out through DNS/TLS/anything in front of
# this box to the public hostname and back in. Confirmed in production as
# the cause of doubled Apache connection load per page view (one
# held-open browser-facing CGI request plus one self-inflicted extra
# inbound HTTPS request per API call) and the resulting connection
# exhaustion/timeouts under even modest concurrent traffic -- with
# refused connections that never show up in the error log, because Apache
# never accepted them in the first place. If you used `certbot --apache`,
# check the :80 block it generated and add the same exclusion described
# below by hand; certbot has no way to know this project's own API calls
# itself over loopback.
#
# To do it by hand:
#
#   1. WSGIDaemonProcess is a server-wide declaration, not per-vhost --
#      move the `WSGIDaemonProcess planetgen-api ...` line above BOTH
#      VirtualHost blocks (rather than leaving it inside :80's, as the
#      block above has it today) so the :443 block below can reference
#      the same process group too, without a second, conflicting
#      declaration. `WSGIScriptAlias`/`<Directory api>`/`<Files
#      "wsgi.py">` still need to be repeated in EACH vhost that mounts
#      /api (mod_wsgi's own per-vhost mount, unlike the daemon process
#      itself) -- copy those three from the :80 block above into the new
#      :443 block, along with everything else in it (`<Directory html>`/
#      `<Directory lib>`/`<Directory static>` etc.), then add:
#
#        SSLEngine on
#        SSLCertificateFile /etc/letsencrypt/live/planetgen.example.com/fullchain.pem
#        SSLCertificateKeyFile /etc/letsencrypt/live/planetgen.example.com/privkey.pem
#
#   2. Replace the `:80` block's contents with just its own `/api` mount
#      (`WSGIScriptAlias`/`<Directory api>`/`<Files "wsgi.py">`, kept
#      as-is) plus a redirect for everything else:
#
#        RewriteEngine On
#        RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/api
#        RewriteRule ^ https://%{SERVER_NAME}%{REQUEST_URI} [R=permanent,L]
#
# Needs `a2enmod ssl rewrite` in addition to the modules enabled above.
